WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
In this lesson, we're looking at why history summarization loses facts and what you can do about it.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
We'll cover which categories of information are most at risk, how loss compounds across turns,

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
and the extract-first pattern that breaks the cycle.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.000
By the end, you'll know exactly what gets dropped and why.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Let's get into it.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.984
Picture a session where the user set a rate limit at turn 4, and by turn 30, the model is quietly

7
00:00:27.984 --> 00:00:29.927
ignoring it. Nothing erred.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.000
What happened is history summarization.

9
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.000
The API doesn't do this for you, and left unmanaged an overlong request simply fails.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:46.000
When history approaches the context window, your application condenses prior turns into a shorter narrative

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.000
that preserves the gist and drops the specifics, and it does that silently.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.000
You don't get a warning.

13
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
The fix is a persistent fact block, a structured region of the prompt that holds extracted facts

14
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
and survives the compression pass intact.

15
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Getting facts into that block is the job of fact extraction,

16
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.000
a step that runs against the raw turn before the summarizer can consume it.

17
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Everything else in this lesson builds on that arrangement.

18
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Summarization exists for a good reason.

19
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Long conversations accumulate more tokens than the context window can hold, so something has to give.

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Your application compresses older turns into a shorter narrative.

21
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
The tradeoff is that fluency improves and specificity drops.

22
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
The compressed version reads naturally and preserves the overall flow of the conversation.

23
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
What it loses are the exact values, the specific number you mentioned,

24
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.000
the exact name of the tool you chose, the precise threshold you set.

25
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
And here's what makes this particularly tricky.

26
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
A home-rolled summarizer doesn't flag what it dropped.

27
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The conversation just continues as if everything is still intact.

28
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Claude does offer an opt-in server-side compaction feature, and that one returns a visible compaction block.

29
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Silent loss is what we design around, and it causes most long-session reliability failures.

30
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Let's make the tradeoff concrete with a comparison.

31
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
On the fluency side, the compressed summary reads cleanly, and the model can continue responding coherently.

32
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Relationships between ideas stay intact, and the overall arc of the conversation is preserved.

33
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
That's genuinely useful.

34
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.000
On the specificity side, the exact number you entered for a rate limit is gone.

35
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.000
The name of the specific data set you referenced is gone.

36
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
The decision you made three turns ago about which approach to use is gone.

37
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:50.462
The model has no way to distinguish between a token budget of $512 and one of $8,192 if the specific

38
00:02:50.462 --> 00:02:52.925
value was dropped in compression.

39
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.520
That distinction matters enormously when your application depends on it, and the model won't tell

40
00:02:58.520 --> 00:02:59.943
you the value is missing.

41
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Not all information is equally vulnerable to summarization loss.

42
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Think in four categories. Identifiers go first.

43
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.609
User IDs, ticket numbers, version strings, and session tokens look like opaque strings to a

44
00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:15.938
summarizer, but they're critical to application logic.

45
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Numeric limits are next.

46
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.389
Token budgets, rate limits, retry thresholds, and cost caps frequently get compressed down to

47
00:03:24.389 --> 00:03:26.931
something like a limit was mentioned.

48
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Named entities, meaning specific people, tools, data sets, and services get generalized or dropped entirely.

49
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.106
And decisions, the choices that constrain later steps, get softened into a vague narrative about the

50
00:03:41.106 --> 00:03:42.938
direction of the conversation.

51
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.907
These four categories are where you should focus your extraction logic, because they're what the

52
00:03:48.907 --> 00:03:50.938
summarizer consistently discards.

53
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.000
You can recognize fact loss from its symptoms even before you know the root cause.

54
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Constraint amnesia is when a rule the user stated early in the session gets silently ignored several turns later.

55
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.000
The model doesn't say it forgot the rule, it just stops applying it.

56
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Entity confusion is subtler.

57
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:17.000
The model substitutes a plausible name or number for the one the user actually provided.

58
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.000
It sounds confident, and it's wrong.

59
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:25.000
Decision drift is when a committed choice gets reopened as if it were still under discussion.

60
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:33.000
The user said, we're using approach A, and three turns later, the model is evaluating approach B again.

61
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.000
All three symptoms trace back to the same root cause.

62
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Facts that weren't extracted before summarization consumed them.

63
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Here's what makes this problem worse over time.

64
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Each summarization pass starts from the output of the previous one.

65
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.000
The first pass compresses raw turns and drops minor details.

66
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.000
The second pass compresses a summary and drops a bit more.

67
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.000
The third pass compresses a summary of a summary.

68
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
By that point, only the broadest narrative survives.

69
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.000
This is compounding loss, and it accelerates faster than most developers expect.

70
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.000
A constraint stated clearly at turn 5 might be completely gone by turn 20.

71
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.872
Not because any single pass was careless, but because each pass had a little less to work with than

72
00:05:20.872 --> 00:05:21.940
the one before it.

73
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Long sessions don't just get longer.

74
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.000
They behave qualitatively differently from short ones, and you need to design for that explicitly.

75
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.000
The extract first pattern is a simple reordering of operations that breaks the loss cycle entirely.

76
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Instead of summarizing a turn and then trying to recover what was lost, you extract first.

77
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:50.000
Step 1. As a new turn arrives, run a fact extraction pass against the raw turn text before anything else happens.

78
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Step 2. Write the extracted facts into the persistent block.

79
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Step 3. Summarize the turn for the rolling history.

80
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.250
How the block absorbs each new fact is a separate update strategy decision, and that is exactly what

81
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:05.937
the next lesson builds out.

82
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
What matters here is the ordering.

83
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Extraction runs against the raw turn before compression touches it,

84
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.000
so the summarizer no longer has sole custody of the facts your application depends on.

85
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Extract first, summarize second is the ordering that survives every long session you'll ever build.

86
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Sequence is the whole trick.

87
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:34.000
The moment a summarizer runs, it optimizes for reading well, and specifics are the cheapest thing to drop.

88
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Pull the facts out before that happens, and compression can be as aggressive as it needs to be,

89
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.000
because the details are already saved.

90
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.000
That's where we'll leave this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

